Verdict: MAJOR  
Retrieval adequacy: insufficient

Findings:
- [major] The plan’s chosen discoverability surfaces omit the required Codex adapter and are not aligned with the control-plane contract it cites. In `Artifact Map` and `Files to Change`, the anchors are `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `GEMINI.md`, and `docs/standards/CONTROL_PLANE_CONTRACT.md`, but `docs/standards/CONTROL_PLANE_CONTRACT.md` says provider-specific adapters live in `.claude/`, `.codex/`, and `.gemini/`, and marks `.codex/` and `.gemini/` as required adapter directories. The plan never updates `.codex/` or explains why Codex is excluded even though `config/agents/codex/config.toml` is listed under “Existing repo code.”
- [major] The plan is not approval-ready because it leaves a core scope decision unresolved: `Risks and Open Questions` asks, “should CODEX.md also be introduced as a root thin adapter...?” That is not a harmless follow-up; it directly affects the contract boundary and discoverability-anchor design for one of the named providers. A plan cannot be approved while still undecided on whether it is documenting current state or inventing a new adapter convention.
- [major] Retrieval is incomplete relative to the repo’s own planning contract. `docs/plans/README.md` requires the union of issue-class retrieval bundles; for documentation work that includes governance docs in the target directory and the durable-vs-transient boundary policy (#2209), and for harness work it includes `CONTROL_PLANE_CONTRACT.md`, `config/agents/`, and `.claude/rules/`. The plan explicitly records only the harness retrieval requirement under “Standards” and never cites #2209 or any documentation-boundary source, despite proposing changes to `docs/standards/*`, `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, and `GEMINI.md`.
- [major] The TDD section is underspecified to the point of being non-executable. `TDD Test List` names tests like `test_contract_covers_context_budget_and_truncation_safety`, but gives no test file, harness, parser, or command that would make these checks real. Given the repo-level hard gate “TDD mandatory” in `AGENTS.md`, this is not enough to prove feasibility.
- [major] The plan proposes editing three harness files without any guard for the repo’s 20-line hard limit. `.claude/rules/coding-style.md` says `CLAUDE.md, MEMORY.md, AGENTS.md, GEMINI.md must not exceed 20 lines`. `Files to Change` includes `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, and `GEMINI.md`, but no test or acceptance criterion verifies those files remain within limit after adding anchors. That is a predictable failure mode the plan should already cover.

Missing tests:
- A line-count compliance test for `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, and `GEMINI.md` against `.claude/rules/coding-style.md`.
- A concrete link-anchor validation test that checks the exact chosen paths, including the Codex adapter decision, not just generic “explicit links/references present.”
- A test or lint step that proves the new contract/playbook does not contradict `docs/standards/CONTROL_PLANE_CONTRACT.md`.
- A scope-boundary test that rejects tier-1 inventory language in all new docs, not just in the summary package.

Scope creep concerns:
- `Files to Change` includes `docs/plans/README.md`, which is workflow bookkeeping for plan indexing, not part of the issue’s stated deliverables.
- The open question about introducing `CODEX.md` risks turning a documentation-contract issue into an adapter-architecture change.

Weakest assumption:
- That root `CLAUDE.md` and `GEMINI.md` are the right canonical provider anchors for this issue while `.codex/` can be omitted, despite `CONTROL_PLANE_CONTRACT.md` defining the adapter directories as the provider-specific surfaces.

Most likely implementation failure mode:
- The docs land with anchors in `AGENTS.md`/root adapters only, leaving Codex undiscoverable or inconsistently handled relative to the control-plane contract.

Most likely test gap:
- Document-presence tests pass while the actual contract remains internally inconsistent with the required adapter topology and file-length constraints.

Future issues suggested:
- A separate issue to normalize provider discoverability surfaces if the repo wants root-level Codex parity rather than the current `.codex/`-only adapter model.
- A small enforcement issue to add automated line-limit and anchor-validation checks for `AGENTS.md`/provider adapter entrypoints.

Review confidence: high
